icom: read frames into the caller's buffer, not MAXFRAMELEN

read_icom_frame() told read_string() that every buffer holds MAXFRAMELEN (200)
bytes, and read_string() clears that many before it reads. One caller has
less: icom_get_usb_echo_off(), run by rig_open() for every Icom-protocol rig,
reads into 16 bytes once it has seen its command echoed. 184 bytes of its
stack frame were zeroed then, and a long frame from the rig would have been
written there. (AddressSanitizer reports it in every scenario of
HamlibIcomTuningTest.)

diff --git a/rigs/icom/frame.c b/rigs/icom/frame.c
--- a/rigs/icom/frame.c
+++ b/rigs/icom/frame.c
@@ -485,14 +485,17 @@ static int read_icom_frame_generic(hamlib_port_t *p,
     {
         int i;
 
+        /* [RADE] The size is the caller's, not MAXFRAMELEN: read_string()
+         * clears and fills as many bytes as it is told, and
+         * icom_get_usb_echo_off() reads into 16 bytes. */
         if (direct)
         {
-            i = read_string_direct(p, rx_ptr, MAXFRAMELEN - read,
+            i = read_string_direct(p, rx_ptr, rxbuffer_len - read,
                                    icom_block_end, icom_block_end_length, 0, 1);
         }
         else
         {
-            i = read_string(p, rx_ptr, MAXFRAMELEN - read,
+            i = read_string(p, rx_ptr, rxbuffer_len - read,
                             icom_block_end, icom_block_end_length, 0, 1);
         }
 
